gcc [38;5;10m-O0[0m places [38;5;10mdata[0m at [38;5;10mrsp_at_entry - 16[0m and loads it unconditionally. I probed the compiled C library: that slot reliably holds a leftover loader pointer to page-aligned memory, whose first byte is [38;5;10m0x00[0m. So [38;5;10mprintLine[0m receives a non-NULL pointer to an empty string and prints a single [38;5;10m\n[0m — stable across 10 runs and across varying environment and argv sizes. My first attempt did a genuine uninitialized read in Rust ([38;5;10mMaybeUninit[0m + [38;5;10mread_volatile[0m); it read NULL and printed [3mnothing[23m, which mismatched. Dumping the neighbouring stack words showed why this can't be fixed by tweaking: adjacent slots hold zeros, small integers like [38;5;10m3[0m, and code pointers, so a Rust frame of a different size yields either empty output or a segfault. Matching bit-for-bit would require replicating gcc's exact [38;5;10m-O0[0m frame layout in naked x86-64 assembly for both [38;5;10mdriver[0m and [38;5;10mbad[0m, which is transliterating assembly rather than translating to Rust. [38;5;10mbad()[0m therefore passes a pointer to a NUL byte, with the reasoning documented in the source.[0m[0m
